1
00:00:48,006 --> 00:00:54,971
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

2
00:01:01,144 --> 00:01:02,145
Who?

3
00:01:02,729 --> 00:01:04,397
I don't know anyone by that name.

4
00:01:04,981 --> 00:01:05,857
"Keum Seo-hee."

5
00:01:08,651 --> 00:01:11,488
Don't you know
what that name means to So-ran?

6
00:01:13,823 --> 00:01:15,075
Her husband,

7
00:01:16,868 --> 00:01:18,536
the man you met today,

8
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:20,789
refused to turn in his comrades.

9
00:01:20,872 --> 00:01:23,208
That's why Sato killed his daughter.

10
00:01:26,211 --> 00:01:27,837
Right in front of So-ran's eyes.

11
00:01:32,675 --> 00:01:34,844
Even if our cause
doesn't make sense to you,

12
00:01:36,596 --> 00:01:37,555
think about So-ran.

13
00:01:39,349 --> 00:01:41,684
Can't you understand her goal?

14
00:01:43,645 --> 00:01:45,313
I know you think it's crazy.

15
00:01:45,396 --> 00:01:47,190
But if one lost
their beloved child overnight,

16
00:01:47,273 --> 00:01:49,526
anyone would have done the same.

17
00:01:53,613 --> 00:01:54,906
Please.

18
00:01:55,990 --> 00:01:57,784
Try to understand So-ran.

19
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:32,527
Out of the way.
Traitors are dealt with. Remember?

20
00:02:32,610 --> 00:02:33,778
We need to bring her back.

21
00:02:33,862 --> 00:02:34,779
Why would we do that?

22
00:02:34,863 --> 00:02:36,072
We need to tie up loose ends.

23
00:02:36,156 --> 00:02:38,324
She did her job as a spy properly.

24
00:02:38,408 --> 00:02:39,909
Why should we kill her?

25
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:41,578
We're the ones who didn't trust her.

26
00:02:41,661 --> 00:02:43,580
She just said she's out.

27
00:02:44,497 --> 00:02:45,915
If she goes running her mouth--

28
00:02:45,999 --> 00:02:48,459
She has no intention of betraying us.

29
00:02:49,836 --> 00:02:52,172
She said she's scared
of getting caught and killed.

30
00:02:52,255 --> 00:02:55,466
That means betraying us to save herself
isn't even on her mind.

31
00:02:55,550 --> 00:02:56,885
She's terrified of dying

32
00:02:56,968 --> 00:02:58,386
if her cover is blown.

33
00:02:58,469 --> 00:03:02,599
But even in that situation,
turning on us isn't an option for her.

34
00:03:02,682 --> 00:03:03,516
Don't you get it?

35
00:03:04,934 --> 00:03:07,854
She's the spy we sent to kill Sato.

36
00:03:07,937 --> 00:03:10,231
We shouldn't be trying to kill her
right now.

37
00:03:10,773 --> 00:03:13,193
We should be trying to convince her
to come back.

38
00:03:23,828 --> 00:03:25,205
Just this once.

39
00:03:27,665 --> 00:03:29,626
I won't try to persuade her again.

40
00:04:07,538 --> 00:04:08,748
Hideo.

41
00:04:08,831 --> 00:04:09,999
Hanako?

42
00:04:10,541 --> 00:04:11,376
Hanako.

43
00:04:11,459 --> 00:04:13,378
- Hanako.
- Hideo.

44
00:04:13,461 --> 00:04:14,462
Hanako.

45
00:04:14,545 --> 00:04:16,631
Hideo must be tired. He needs some rest--

46
00:04:16,714 --> 00:04:17,799
Let go!

47
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:20,301
Hideo.

48
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:22,929
I wrote all the capital letters.

49
00:04:23,012 --> 00:04:24,264
Look.

50
00:04:24,347 --> 00:04:27,016
Hanako, I'm sorry.

51
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:29,269
I'm a bit tired today.
How about next time?

52
00:04:29,352 --> 00:04:30,645
Next time? When?

53
00:04:30,728 --> 00:04:32,814
You always come home late.

54
00:04:32,897 --> 00:04:36,067
Look. I memorized all the capital letters.

55
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:36,985
Hanako.

56
00:04:37,568 --> 00:04:39,487
Maybe tomorrow. I promise I'll look.

57
00:04:39,570 --> 00:04:41,114
You're a liar!

58
00:04:42,198 --> 00:04:44,742
You always come late,
and you always say, "Maybe tomorrow."

59
00:04:44,826 --> 00:04:47,036
You're not going to keep your promise!

60
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:48,955
I need some space today, okay?

61
00:04:54,585 --> 00:04:56,087
I hate you!

62
00:05:09,559 --> 00:05:10,893
Don't worry about Hanako.

63
00:05:10,977 --> 00:05:12,353
I'll go check on her.

64
00:05:12,437 --> 00:05:13,521
Good night.

65
00:05:27,452 --> 00:05:28,953
You insolent <i>Josenjing!</i>

66
00:05:31,914 --> 00:05:32,749
Come in.

67
00:05:36,252 --> 00:05:37,086
Father.

68
00:05:40,048 --> 00:05:41,007
Well done.

69
00:05:41,758 --> 00:05:42,884
Sorry?

70
00:05:42,967 --> 00:05:43,885
What do you mean?

71
00:05:46,387 --> 00:05:47,889
Even if you're slapped,

72
00:05:47,972 --> 00:05:50,808
a true Japanese must show no emotion.

73
00:06:02,653 --> 00:06:04,322
Sit upright, Hideo.

74
00:06:06,199 --> 00:06:07,784
Japanese people don't sit like that.

75
00:06:10,828 --> 00:06:13,664
From now on,
never let your thoughts or feelings

76
00:06:14,332 --> 00:06:15,666
show on your face.

77
00:06:16,709 --> 00:06:18,586
That's what makes you Japanese

78
00:06:18,669 --> 00:06:19,837
and my son.

79
00:06:20,421 --> 00:06:21,672
Do you understand, Hideo?

80
00:06:26,469 --> 00:06:27,303
Answer me.

81
00:06:28,054 --> 00:06:29,555
Yes, Father.

82
00:06:31,682 --> 00:06:34,227
Father.

83
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:36,437
<i>Well done.</i>

84
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:39,774
Thank you.

85
00:06:39,857 --> 00:06:42,944
You didn't think Fukuda
would get much out of questioning him,

86
00:06:43,444 --> 00:06:44,570
did you?

87
00:06:45,822 --> 00:06:46,739
No.

88
00:06:46,823 --> 00:06:48,616
Interpreter trainee Keum Seo-hee
was called in

89
00:06:48,699 --> 00:06:50,535
and didn't return for seven hours.

90
00:06:51,285 --> 00:06:53,329
If it'd gone well,
it wouldn't have taken that long.

91
00:06:55,623 --> 00:06:56,749
You have a point.

92
00:06:58,626 --> 00:06:59,669
Keum Seo-hee.

93
00:07:00,878 --> 00:07:01,712
Yes?

94
00:07:02,839 --> 00:07:04,549
Did you make sure she won't speak?

95
00:07:05,842 --> 00:07:08,553
If Yoo Philip writes an article,
it'll be a problem.

96
00:07:09,595 --> 00:07:10,430
Okay.

97
00:07:12,682 --> 00:07:14,517
She's going to rely on you a lot.

98
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:17,770
Even if she's dating an American reporter,

99
00:07:18,813 --> 00:07:21,399
she's one of the few women
in the Government-General

100
00:07:21,482 --> 00:07:22,567
and a Joseon person at that.

101
00:07:23,568 --> 00:07:24,694
I don't really follow…

102
00:07:24,777 --> 00:07:27,196
I assume everything
she sees, hears, and thinks

103
00:07:28,114 --> 00:07:29,824
is passed on to Mr. Yoo.

104
00:07:31,325 --> 00:07:33,202
Get her to rely on you

105
00:07:33,286 --> 00:07:34,620
and trust you.

106
00:07:37,582 --> 00:07:38,708
But keep this in mind.

107
00:07:39,917 --> 00:07:41,419
She needs to trust you,

108
00:07:42,170 --> 00:07:44,589
but you must never trust her.

109
00:07:46,424 --> 00:07:48,384
Joseon people are an untrustworthy bunch.

110
00:07:49,343 --> 00:07:50,386
And Joseon women

111
00:07:51,804 --> 00:07:53,389
are bound to betray us.

112
00:07:57,143 --> 00:07:58,728
That's exactly what your mother did.

113
00:08:01,230 --> 00:08:03,191
She promised to spend her life with me,

114
00:08:05,526 --> 00:08:06,569
only to leave me.

115
00:08:45,566 --> 00:08:47,318
<i>I know you think it's crazy.</i>

116
00:08:47,401 --> 00:08:49,362
<i>But if one lost</i>
<i>their beloved child overnight,</i>

117
00:08:49,445 --> 00:08:51,948
<i>anyone would have done the same.</i>

118
00:08:54,742 --> 00:08:55,743
Anyone…

119
00:08:56,994 --> 00:08:58,329
Her damn mother

120
00:08:58,412 --> 00:09:01,707
dumped her own kid
to sleep around with a Jap.

121
00:09:02,917 --> 00:09:03,751
- A Jap?
- With a Jap…

122
00:09:03,834 --> 00:09:06,379
<i>When my mom left me</i>…

123
00:09:09,549 --> 00:09:11,509
<i>I resented her a lot, naturally.</i>

124
00:09:12,802 --> 00:09:16,764
<i>But as time went on,</i>
<i>I started telling myself…</i>

125
00:09:18,599 --> 00:09:19,892
<i>that my mom</i>

126
00:09:20,893 --> 00:09:23,521
<i>had just found someone she loved more,</i>

127
00:09:24,272 --> 00:09:26,274
<i>but that she still loved me too.</i>

128
00:09:29,151 --> 00:09:30,027
<i>So…</i>

129
00:09:31,612 --> 00:09:34,740
<i>I was just a little unlucky.</i>

130
00:09:35,533 --> 00:09:37,243
<i>I wasn't a pitiful kid.</i>

131
00:09:37,994 --> 00:09:42,206
<i>That's what I've believed all this time.</i>

132
00:09:46,919 --> 00:09:48,462
<i>But seeing you,</i>

133
00:09:50,214 --> 00:09:54,218
<i>I find myself a little jealous of Seo-hee.</i>

134
00:10:01,017 --> 00:10:04,061
<i>Because her mother is willing</i>
<i>to risk her life</i>

135
00:10:05,062 --> 00:10:06,772
<i>for her daughter.</i>

136
00:10:29,837 --> 00:10:30,838
Did you sleep well?

137
00:10:34,717 --> 00:10:36,552
Assassinating the inspector general.

138
00:10:37,303 --> 00:10:39,889
How long will you need?
If you move quickly.

139
00:10:42,767 --> 00:10:43,601
A month.

140
00:10:47,313 --> 00:10:48,689
All right, one month.

141
00:10:49,982 --> 00:10:50,941
That's it.

142
00:10:52,443 --> 00:10:53,319
After one month,

143
00:10:53,861 --> 00:10:56,030
send me to America, no matter what.

144
00:10:58,449 --> 00:10:59,325
Okay.

145
00:10:59,867 --> 00:11:00,993
I promise.

146
00:11:02,328 --> 00:11:03,162
Thank you.

147
00:11:05,873 --> 00:11:07,333
Just one month. Don't forget.

148
00:11:20,012 --> 00:11:26,435
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

149
00:11:26,519 --> 00:11:27,520
JONGNO POLICE STATION

150
00:11:27,603 --> 00:11:29,063
EPISODE 6

151
00:11:36,028 --> 00:11:37,071
Zip it!

152
00:11:38,489 --> 00:11:39,907
Still alive?

153
00:11:39,990 --> 00:11:41,242
They're like cockroaches.

154
00:12:00,469 --> 00:12:02,471
<i>I looked into the thing you asked about.</i>

155
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:05,683
I managed to get in touch
with the Gugukdan.

156
00:12:05,766 --> 00:12:08,310
It turns out the Gugukdan
was planning to kill Sato

157
00:12:08,811 --> 00:12:11,605
that very day we went
to the American reporter's house.

158
00:12:12,231 --> 00:12:15,609
I'm not sure if the American reporter
was part of the Gugukdan,

159
00:12:15,693 --> 00:12:17,153
but I did find out one thing.

160
00:12:17,820 --> 00:12:20,114
They've got a female sniper
in their group.

161
00:12:21,490 --> 00:12:23,617
They say she's as good as Nam Ja-hyeon.

162
00:12:24,618 --> 00:12:26,537
But I don't know her name or any details.

163
00:12:35,755 --> 00:12:38,174
Did you know your fellow members
were killed in Dongsung-dong?

164
00:13:23,511 --> 00:13:24,470
You're all set.

165
00:13:25,471 --> 00:13:26,514
Thank you.

166
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:34,188
So, about that man.

167
00:13:35,105 --> 00:13:36,482
Seo-hee's father.

168
00:13:37,274 --> 00:13:39,985
I'll find a way.

169
00:13:41,445 --> 00:13:42,655
Find a way for what?

170
00:13:45,157 --> 00:13:48,160
He tried to assassinate the inspector
general of the Government-General.

171
00:13:48,244 --> 00:13:49,328
We know how the trial will end.

172
00:13:50,663 --> 00:13:52,873
Even if he's lucky enough
to avoid execution,

173
00:13:52,957 --> 00:13:55,709
how long can he hold out
with those injuries?

174
00:13:55,793 --> 00:13:57,169
Even perfectly healthy men

175
00:13:57,253 --> 00:13:59,797
come out of that prison in body bags.

176
00:14:09,515 --> 00:14:10,933
We don't have time to waste.

177
00:14:11,016 --> 00:14:13,811
We have to act now
to keep them from torturing him.

178
00:14:13,894 --> 00:14:15,104
I'll go talk to Sato.

179
00:14:15,187 --> 00:14:16,772
- Once I meet him--
- Once you do,

180
00:14:18,524 --> 00:14:19,692
what are you planning to do?

181
00:14:19,775 --> 00:14:22,695
I'll tell him I'm interested
in the Kyeam-ri incident.

182
00:14:24,196 --> 00:14:26,574
I could ask to speak
with one of the prisoners.

183
00:14:26,657 --> 00:14:30,160
Once that happens, even Sato
will have to think twice before acting.

184
00:14:34,915 --> 00:14:37,001
What Sato wants to hide the most

185
00:14:37,084 --> 00:14:39,795
is the Kyeam-ri massacre
being brought back into the spotlight.

186
00:14:43,632 --> 00:14:45,384
The mastermind
of the Kyeam-ri massacre, Sato,

187
00:14:45,467 --> 00:14:46,719
no, Ishikawa,

188
00:14:47,553 --> 00:14:50,890
leveraged his in-laws' connections
to become the inspector general.

189
00:14:50,973 --> 00:14:55,185
He even took his wife's family name, Sato,
to claw his way up to the top.

190
00:14:55,269 --> 00:14:58,939
So, with the world's eyes
on Japan right now because of Manchukuo,

191
00:14:59,023 --> 00:15:01,984
if Sato thinks I might publish
the Kyeam-ri story--

192
00:15:02,067 --> 00:15:03,110
And then what?

193
00:15:08,532 --> 00:15:09,992
What difference will it make?

194
00:15:11,243 --> 00:15:13,913
If the foreign press picks it up
with Manchukuo at stake,

195
00:15:13,996 --> 00:15:16,790
Sato might have to step down
as inspector general.

196
00:15:16,874 --> 00:15:18,334
But even if that happens,

197
00:15:19,376 --> 00:15:21,128
is that what we're fighting for?

198
00:15:21,211 --> 00:15:22,379
Is that enough?

199
00:15:25,549 --> 00:15:28,344
If I kill Sato, I can die with no regrets.

200
00:15:28,427 --> 00:15:31,430
But remember,
that's when your fight begins.

201
00:15:32,932 --> 00:15:34,767
You pledged to expose the truth

202
00:15:34,850 --> 00:15:37,186
about Japan's and Sato's atrocities
under your name

203
00:15:37,269 --> 00:15:39,063
and make sure the whole world sees it.

204
00:15:39,146 --> 00:15:41,440
So until then,
you have to maintain good terms

205
00:15:41,523 --> 00:15:44,193
with the Government-General
as an American journalist.

206
00:15:48,614 --> 00:15:52,076
If Sato realizes he was one of the victims
from the Kyeam-ri massacre,

207
00:15:52,159 --> 00:15:54,620
there's a chance he'll find out who I am.

208
00:15:56,372 --> 00:15:58,165
He'll probably keep quiet,

209
00:15:58,248 --> 00:15:59,458
but you never know.

210
00:16:03,754 --> 00:16:07,091
So leave that to fate
and don't do anything.

211
00:16:07,174 --> 00:16:09,593
Even if he dies in prison,

212
00:16:10,219 --> 00:16:11,595
there's nothing we can do.

213
00:16:11,679 --> 00:16:14,348
But, So-ran, he's your husband.

214
00:16:14,431 --> 00:16:15,265
No.

215
00:16:18,227 --> 00:16:20,270
You are my husband.

216
00:16:44,378 --> 00:16:45,504
SATO HIDEO
KEUM SEO-HEE

217
00:16:45,587 --> 00:16:47,172
<i>Win Sato Hideo's heart.</i>

218
00:16:48,215 --> 00:16:49,675
Get him on your side.

219
00:16:50,175 --> 00:16:52,678
The inspector general
keeps people at arm's length.

220
00:16:52,761 --> 00:16:55,097
But Sato Hideo
is one of the few people close to him.

221
00:16:55,681 --> 00:16:56,598
All right.

222
00:16:56,682 --> 00:16:57,850
But remember.

223
00:16:57,933 --> 00:17:00,269
Never let yourself have personal feelings.

224
00:17:01,145 --> 00:17:02,187
<i>Don't worry.</i>

225
00:17:02,938 --> 00:17:04,857
<i>That's never going to happen.</i>

226
00:17:07,568 --> 00:17:08,610
Hello…

227
00:17:32,885 --> 00:17:34,762
<i>Get her to rely on you</i>

228
00:17:34,845 --> 00:17:36,180
<i>and trust you.</i>

229
00:17:36,889 --> 00:17:38,015
<i>But keep this in mind.</i>

230
00:17:38,515 --> 00:17:40,100
<i>She needs to trust you,</i>

231
00:17:40,768 --> 00:17:43,645
<i>but you must never trust her.</i>

232
00:18:20,349 --> 00:18:21,558
There's nothing special.

233
00:18:38,784 --> 00:18:39,660
What is that?

234
00:18:55,300 --> 00:18:56,468
You're here?

235
00:19:03,642 --> 00:19:05,269
<i>"The inspector general's secretary."</i>

236
00:19:06,186 --> 00:19:07,896
"Yamada Hanako."

237
00:19:08,689 --> 00:19:10,691
"The niece
of the Internal Affairs Bureau director."

238
00:19:11,358 --> 00:19:13,527
"She can't speak the Joseon language."

239
00:19:13,610 --> 00:19:15,612
"Sloppy with work."

240
00:19:16,446 --> 00:19:17,990
"A good kid at heart."

241
00:19:19,992 --> 00:19:21,994
"She seems to have a crush on Sato Hideo."

242
00:19:25,205 --> 00:19:27,332
Wait, why on earth?

243
00:19:32,337 --> 00:19:33,589
Hello.

244
00:19:35,048 --> 00:19:37,050
Ms. Yamada,
the inspector general's secretary?

245
00:19:37,134 --> 00:19:38,302
Yes.

246
00:19:41,013 --> 00:19:42,890
Is Mr. Sato in?

247
00:19:43,765 --> 00:19:45,309
He hasn't come in yet.

248
00:19:45,893 --> 00:19:47,102
Not yet?

249
00:19:48,061 --> 00:19:49,021
When will he be in?

250
00:19:49,104 --> 00:19:51,607
Well, I'm not sure.

251
00:19:53,192 --> 00:19:55,110
Should I tell him you stopped by?

252
00:19:55,194 --> 00:19:56,153
No.

253
00:19:57,070 --> 00:19:58,280
Just give him this.

254
00:20:00,199 --> 00:20:02,743
We received a lot of cookies
at the Inspector General's Office.

255
00:20:04,786 --> 00:20:07,456
Please give these to Mr. Sato.

256
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:10,083
Sure. I'll make sure he gets them.

257
00:20:10,167 --> 00:20:11,793
Thank you.

258
00:20:33,398 --> 00:20:34,566
Just one.

259
00:20:52,042 --> 00:20:53,543
LADIES' ROOM

260
00:20:58,382 --> 00:21:00,509
He says he can't put up with this.

261
00:21:01,134 --> 00:21:02,094
NO ENTRY

262
00:21:02,177 --> 00:21:03,178
Go ahead.

263
00:21:24,032 --> 00:21:24,866
Translate it.

264
00:21:34,793 --> 00:21:37,004
The Joseon workers
are being worked too hard,

265
00:21:37,087 --> 00:21:38,797
and they're falling ill one after another.

266
00:21:38,880 --> 00:21:41,174
He says pushing them too hard
to meet the deadline

267
00:21:41,758 --> 00:21:43,343
will backfire on us.

268
00:21:43,427 --> 00:21:45,012
So he wants their working hours reduced

269
00:21:45,095 --> 00:21:48,140
and enough time for rest.

270
00:21:49,558 --> 00:21:52,102
How many hours a day are they working?

271
00:21:52,185 --> 00:21:55,022
Fourteen hours. It's been
three months straight without a day off.

272
00:21:57,399 --> 00:21:59,151
Cut their hours by an hour
every other day.

273
00:22:00,485 --> 00:22:01,320
But…

274
00:22:02,738 --> 00:22:04,990
if they miss the deadline,

275
00:22:05,073 --> 00:22:07,117
they'll be held accountable.

276
00:22:07,951 --> 00:22:08,785
Got it.

277
00:22:18,378 --> 00:22:19,212
Well, then.

278
00:22:20,339 --> 00:22:21,173
Let's go.

279
00:22:49,326 --> 00:22:50,786
Interpreter Sato.

280
00:22:50,869 --> 00:22:51,703
Yes, sir.

281
00:22:54,164 --> 00:22:56,249
Did you interpret that correctly today?

282
00:22:58,168 --> 00:22:59,544
What makes you think I didn't?

283
00:23:02,172 --> 00:23:03,340
All right, I trust you.

284
00:23:09,846 --> 00:23:12,682
Have the workers work
an hour longer every day.

285
00:23:13,683 --> 00:23:14,518
Father.

286
00:23:14,601 --> 00:23:16,603
Why should we go easy on them?

287
00:23:17,854 --> 00:23:19,439
They must meet the deadline.

288
00:23:20,524 --> 00:23:21,650
That's an order,

289
00:23:22,150 --> 00:23:23,068
Interpreter Sato.

290
00:23:32,953 --> 00:23:35,372
INTERPRETER SATO HIDEO

291
00:23:47,426 --> 00:23:48,802
You're here?

292
00:23:50,720 --> 00:23:53,056
I was doing some cleaning.

293
00:23:53,140 --> 00:23:54,933
You weren't coming,
and I had nothing else to do.

294
00:23:55,016 --> 00:23:56,685
So I figured I might as well clean.

295
00:23:59,062 --> 00:24:01,314
By the way, where have you been?

296
00:24:02,065 --> 00:24:04,276
I was worried
since you didn't come in without a word.

297
00:24:05,777 --> 00:24:06,945
Look at all this dust.

298
00:24:07,946 --> 00:24:09,448
If I don't wipe it down,

299
00:24:09,531 --> 00:24:12,200
we'll end up breathing all of it in.

300
00:24:21,168 --> 00:24:22,002
Ms. Keum.

301
00:24:23,712 --> 00:24:24,546
Yes?

302
00:24:26,965 --> 00:24:29,634
Yesterday was hard on you, wasn't it?

303
00:24:30,552 --> 00:24:31,386
Sorry?

304
00:24:31,970 --> 00:24:33,930
You won't be called in
for anything like that again.

305
00:24:36,433 --> 00:24:38,768
It's okay. I wouldn't mind doing it again.

306
00:24:39,728 --> 00:24:40,562
What?

307
00:24:41,438 --> 00:24:44,024
When I got home and thought about it,

308
00:24:44,816 --> 00:24:46,359
I realized I was angry at him.

309
00:24:46,443 --> 00:24:48,278
I nearly died because of him too.

310
00:24:48,987 --> 00:24:49,988
I was startled,

311
00:24:50,071 --> 00:24:53,325
but that was because I faced the man
who tried to kill me before.

312
00:24:53,408 --> 00:24:54,868
I'm okay now.

313
00:24:54,951 --> 00:24:55,952
Just a little tired.

314
00:24:57,454 --> 00:24:59,664
If I get called up
for another interrogation,

315
00:24:59,748 --> 00:25:01,082
I want to do a better job.

316
00:25:03,126 --> 00:25:07,297
I didn't mention anything to Mr. Yoo,
as you asked,

317
00:25:07,380 --> 00:25:08,632
so don't worry.

318
00:25:14,179 --> 00:25:15,722
Oh, one more thing.

319
00:25:15,805 --> 00:25:20,477
You haven't found
that Jongno Pawnshop yet, have you?

320
00:25:22,354 --> 00:25:25,065
I'll help you find it.

321
00:25:25,148 --> 00:25:27,108
I know Jongno like the back of my hand.

322
00:25:27,192 --> 00:25:28,193
You can count on me.

323
00:25:28,777 --> 00:25:29,861
I'll help you.

324
00:25:33,240 --> 00:25:34,074
No thanks.

325
00:25:34,824 --> 00:25:35,784
What?

326
00:25:35,867 --> 00:25:37,869
I don't need your help.

327
00:25:38,745 --> 00:25:42,457
I can take care of my own business.
Just give me the pawn ticket you stole.

328
00:25:43,250 --> 00:25:46,002
I've played along with your game
long enough now.

329
00:25:46,086 --> 00:25:47,921
Now bring me my pawn ticket.

330
00:25:53,176 --> 00:25:54,010
Come in.

331
00:26:00,308 --> 00:26:01,184
Excuse me.

332
00:26:01,268 --> 00:26:05,647
The chief of police sent me to bring
Ms. Keum Seo-hee to Jongno Police Station.

333
00:26:05,730 --> 00:26:08,400
It's for the interrogation
of the same person from yesterday.

334
00:26:11,361 --> 00:26:12,612
Good for you.

335
00:26:12,696 --> 00:26:14,823
Have a nice time.

336
00:26:14,906 --> 00:26:17,158
You said
you wanted to do better next time.

337
00:26:18,535 --> 00:26:19,869
Well, actually…

338
00:26:21,204 --> 00:26:23,832
he told me
to bring you along as well, sir.

339
00:26:37,929 --> 00:26:38,847
Chief.

340
00:26:40,140 --> 00:26:43,184
I thought we'd agreed
that the Foreign Affairs Division

341
00:26:43,268 --> 00:26:45,770
would no longer provide interpreters
for interrogations.

342
00:26:46,354 --> 00:26:49,733
We tried another interpreter,
but he kept asking for Keum Seo-hee.

343
00:26:50,442 --> 00:26:51,985
What else could I do?

344
00:26:53,862 --> 00:26:58,325
Did you two hit it off yesterday
while I wasn't looking?

345
00:26:58,408 --> 00:27:00,285
That is completely inappropriate, Chief.

346
00:27:01,119 --> 00:27:02,203
Otherwise,

347
00:27:02,287 --> 00:27:05,248
why would he be asking for Keum Seo-hee?

348
00:27:05,332 --> 00:27:07,792
We already have
an interpreter at the station.

349
00:27:07,876 --> 00:27:08,835
So why ask for her?

350
00:27:08,918 --> 00:27:12,255
You asked for Ms. Keum yourself
yesterday as well,

351
00:27:13,340 --> 00:27:15,300
when there's an interpreter here.

352
00:27:15,383 --> 00:27:16,509
Didn't you?

353
00:27:17,927 --> 00:27:21,139
We'll just have to find out
why he specifically requested

354
00:27:21,222 --> 00:27:22,807
interpreter trainee Keum Seo-hee.

355
00:27:23,475 --> 00:27:24,517
Fair enough.

356
00:27:25,518 --> 00:27:29,522
The inspector general heard about this

357
00:27:30,023 --> 00:27:32,150
and ordered Interpreter Sato to sit in

358
00:27:32,233 --> 00:27:35,320
to ensure the interrogation goes smoothly.

359
00:27:35,403 --> 00:27:37,822
I'm truly touched

360
00:27:38,782 --> 00:27:41,159
by his thoughtfulness.

361
00:27:48,416 --> 00:27:49,501
My goodness.

362
00:27:49,584 --> 00:27:50,710
Tell me about it.

363
00:27:51,294 --> 00:27:53,755
Mr. Sato kindly joined me today.

364
00:27:53,838 --> 00:27:56,675
I think I'll be able to do
a better job interpreting.

365
00:27:57,175 --> 00:27:59,260
Interpreter Sato Hideo

366
00:27:59,344 --> 00:28:03,223
speaks Japanese, English,
and the Joseon language, so…

367
00:28:05,266 --> 00:28:06,393
I'm sorry.

368
00:28:07,060 --> 00:28:10,480
I accidentally used
the Joseon language in front of you.

369
00:28:11,064 --> 00:28:12,899
I just said Interpreter Sato Hideo

370
00:28:12,982 --> 00:28:15,944
can speak Japanese, English,
and the Joseon language.

371
00:28:16,027 --> 00:28:18,655
Of course. He's an interpreter, after all.

372
00:28:18,738 --> 00:28:20,740
Yes, that's right.

373
00:28:23,326 --> 00:28:26,621
Since Interpreter Sato Hideo
speaks the Joseon language fluently,

374
00:28:26,705 --> 00:28:31,292
I'll need to be extra careful
with my interpretation.

375
00:28:31,376 --> 00:28:34,087
He'll catch any mistakes right away.

376
00:28:36,548 --> 00:28:37,465
Let's begin.

377
00:28:38,174 --> 00:28:39,008
Yes, sir.

378
00:28:41,970 --> 00:28:44,389
No, I'll observe from the back.

379
00:28:54,107 --> 00:28:55,108
Your name?

380
00:28:55,859 --> 00:28:57,068
What's your name?

381
00:29:00,196 --> 00:29:02,240
Let me ask you again. What's your name?

382
00:29:02,323 --> 00:29:03,825
He's asking you again.

383
00:29:04,451 --> 00:29:05,452
What's your name?

384
00:29:07,495 --> 00:29:09,914
I granted your request,
so shouldn't you cooperate?

385
00:29:09,998 --> 00:29:11,875
I brought your interpreter.

386
00:29:11,958 --> 00:29:14,377
- I'll ask you again. What's your name?
- Answer him.

387
00:29:14,461 --> 00:29:16,629
What's your name?

388
00:29:18,006 --> 00:29:18,882
E…

389
00:29:21,468 --> 00:29:22,552
Go on.

390
00:29:25,513 --> 00:29:28,141
Tell me, what's your name?

391
00:29:30,101 --> 00:29:31,561
Eat…

392
00:29:34,397 --> 00:29:35,523
shit.

393
00:29:35,607 --> 00:29:36,983
You son of a bitch!

394
00:29:45,867 --> 00:29:47,118
Typical <i>Josenjing.</i>

395
00:29:47,202 --> 00:29:49,662
You just run that filthy mouth of yours.

396
00:29:50,747 --> 00:29:53,875
You lot are all the same, <i>Josenjing.</i>

397
00:29:55,877 --> 00:29:58,213
You can try to hide what's inside,

398
00:29:58,296 --> 00:30:00,507
but you stink in the end.

399
00:30:01,007 --> 00:30:04,344
It always gives you away, <i>Josenjings.</i>

400
00:30:07,931 --> 00:30:09,849
Chief, we brought her here.

401
00:30:10,934 --> 00:30:11,810
Oh yeah?

402
00:30:13,061 --> 00:30:14,771
- Go ahead.
- Yes, sir.

403
00:30:30,537 --> 00:30:32,539
Na Byeong-sun, you have a visitor.

404
00:31:07,949 --> 00:31:08,825
Honey.

405
00:31:13,037 --> 00:31:13,872
Honey.

406
00:31:15,206 --> 00:31:16,374
Honey!

407
00:31:18,167 --> 00:31:19,627
Forgive us.

408
00:31:19,711 --> 00:31:22,463
Please forgive us!

409
00:31:23,172 --> 00:31:24,007
Honey.

410
00:31:24,799 --> 00:31:25,758
Honey.

411
00:31:26,384 --> 00:31:29,387
Please forgive us.

412
00:31:32,724 --> 00:31:34,642
Honey!

413
00:31:57,874 --> 00:31:58,708
Young-bok.

414
00:32:03,671 --> 00:32:04,839
Why are you up?

415
00:32:05,757 --> 00:32:07,133
You need to stay in bed.

416
00:32:08,801 --> 00:32:09,636
I'm fine.

417
00:32:09,719 --> 00:32:11,220
I'll go to my room now.

418
00:32:11,930 --> 00:32:13,139
No, don't worry.

419
00:32:13,222 --> 00:32:14,557
My dad has no idea.

420
00:32:17,644 --> 00:32:21,022
The floor must be uncomfortable.
You can't sleep well on the floor.

421
00:32:27,362 --> 00:32:28,321
Fine.

422
00:32:29,697 --> 00:32:31,407
Have some pine-nut porridge before you go.

423
00:32:33,368 --> 00:32:34,327
No, thank you.

424
00:32:36,079 --> 00:32:37,288
I'll just take this.

425
00:32:38,414 --> 00:32:39,540
Well, then.

426
00:32:40,875 --> 00:32:42,001
Thank you.

427
00:32:48,049 --> 00:32:49,592
You silly fool.

428
00:32:50,677 --> 00:32:53,638
Do you think I couldn't sleep
because I was on the floor?

429
00:32:58,309 --> 00:32:59,894
Oh my, the bandages.

430
00:33:18,246 --> 00:33:21,749
DON'T YOU DARE DIE

431
00:33:37,598 --> 00:33:38,433
Please, have some.

432
00:33:39,017 --> 00:33:39,851
Thanks.

433
00:33:49,193 --> 00:33:54,907
How is the pneumatic tube project
coming along?

434
00:33:54,991 --> 00:33:57,118
We're in the final stretch.

435
00:33:58,202 --> 00:34:01,414
Thank you once again
for your generous donation.

436
00:34:03,708 --> 00:34:07,170
It brings immense pride to my family.

437
00:34:07,712 --> 00:34:10,131
It was my lifelong regret
that I couldn't contribute

438
00:34:10,214 --> 00:34:14,677
even a single brick
when this building was being built.

439
00:34:18,056 --> 00:34:20,850
I'm sure you're here to see
how the tube installation is going.

440
00:34:21,684 --> 00:34:23,644
You should have a look.
It's on the first floor.

441
00:34:24,228 --> 00:34:26,147
Wait, Inspector General.

442
00:34:26,230 --> 00:34:27,065
Well…

443
00:34:30,401 --> 00:34:31,569
Here.

444
00:34:33,654 --> 00:34:35,615
This is my daughter.

445
00:34:44,540 --> 00:34:47,335
I was hoping I could have dinner
at home tonight.

446
00:34:51,964 --> 00:34:53,007
Chief.

447
00:34:54,884 --> 00:34:58,429
May I try speaking with him
in our language?

448
00:35:00,932 --> 00:35:02,934
We're both Joseon people,

449
00:35:03,017 --> 00:35:05,561
so I'll see if I can convince him to talk.

450
00:35:05,645 --> 00:35:09,899
What are you people plotting,
speaking your language behind my back?

451
00:35:11,901 --> 00:35:12,860
That's right.

452
00:35:12,944 --> 00:35:16,155
Why don't you two Joseon people
have an intimate conversation?

453
00:35:17,031 --> 00:35:18,574
Should I get you a room?

454
00:35:18,658 --> 00:35:19,909
Chief!

455
00:35:22,078 --> 00:35:23,704
There's no need to worry.

456
00:35:24,997 --> 00:35:27,083
Even if you don't trust me,

457
00:35:27,166 --> 00:35:30,378
Interpreter Sato here
speaks the Joseon language,

458
00:35:30,461 --> 00:35:32,004
and he's keeping an eye on us.

459
00:35:34,423 --> 00:35:35,508
Fine, let's see what you can do.

460
00:35:39,428 --> 00:35:41,264
Thank you, sir.

461
00:35:50,648 --> 00:35:51,566
When you

462
00:35:52,483 --> 00:35:55,695
snuck into the house as a deliveryman,

463
00:35:56,195 --> 00:35:57,363
I was the live-in maid there.

464
00:35:58,239 --> 00:36:00,658
You pointed a gun at me that day.

465
00:36:02,410 --> 00:36:05,663
If you'd ever tried to kill my madam,

466
00:36:05,746 --> 00:36:07,915
I would've found a way to stop you.

467
00:36:07,999 --> 00:36:10,168
That is, if you'd tried to kill her.

468
00:36:13,880 --> 00:36:17,800
She's Chinese, so she doesn't speak
the Joseon language very well.

469
00:36:19,427 --> 00:36:21,596
But she's really good to me.

470
00:36:22,513 --> 00:36:26,809
She gave me the pretty name Keum Seo-hee.

471
00:36:28,394 --> 00:36:29,687
I wonder

472
00:36:29,770 --> 00:36:33,232
how she came up
with such a pretty name for me.

473
00:36:35,818 --> 00:36:37,945
Anyway, I'm completely on her side.

474
00:36:42,658 --> 00:36:43,534
Hey.

475
00:36:44,577 --> 00:36:45,745
You're dragging this out.

476
00:36:45,828 --> 00:36:47,079
What are you saying?

477
00:36:48,164 --> 00:36:49,290
I'm sorry.

478
00:36:49,916 --> 00:36:53,961
I was making small talk
to get him to open up.

479
00:37:00,760 --> 00:37:03,387
Did you know your fellow members
were killed in Dongsung-dong?

480
00:37:06,682 --> 00:37:08,517
Yesterday, in Dongsung-dong…

481
00:37:10,394 --> 00:37:11,979
Did he just say Dongsung-dong?

482
00:37:14,398 --> 00:37:15,608
He's finally talking.

483
00:37:16,359 --> 00:37:17,401
What is he saying?

484
00:37:18,027 --> 00:37:20,071
What happened to the people who got away?

485
00:37:21,530 --> 00:37:23,783
I heard they'd been shot.

486
00:37:25,910 --> 00:37:27,662
What's he saying?

487
00:37:29,580 --> 00:37:32,166
He's asking what happened
to those who were wounded and escaped

488
00:37:32,250 --> 00:37:33,876
yesterday in Dongsung-dong.

489
00:37:35,920 --> 00:37:37,338
I'll try to get him

490
00:37:37,421 --> 00:37:39,465
to talk about something else.

491
00:37:40,424 --> 00:37:41,801
Fine.

492
00:37:43,302 --> 00:37:46,806
You want to know if your comrades
who fled yesterday are safe, right?

493
00:37:50,101 --> 00:37:52,395
Of course. I understand how you feel.

494
00:37:52,478 --> 00:37:54,689
Whenever something dangerous happens,

495
00:37:54,772 --> 00:37:56,857
I immediately worry about my madam first.

496
00:37:57,441 --> 00:38:00,820
When she saw me off this morning,

497
00:38:00,903 --> 00:38:04,991
I just hoped she'd stay safe
and live a long life.

498
00:38:18,963 --> 00:38:22,174
Chief, his accomplice gave us his name.

499
00:38:22,258 --> 00:38:24,635
Is that so?

500
00:38:24,719 --> 00:38:26,304
How did you get it out of him?

501
00:38:26,387 --> 00:38:27,930
We snapped his wife's leg.

502
00:38:37,398 --> 00:38:38,899
You broke her leg?

503
00:38:42,695 --> 00:38:44,947
You damn monsters!

504
00:38:45,865 --> 00:38:47,700
What? He speaks Japanese just fine!

505
00:38:52,955 --> 00:38:54,707
Oh well, it doesn't matter anymore.

506
00:38:57,710 --> 00:38:59,712
- So, what's his name?
- Yes.

507
00:38:59,795 --> 00:39:01,547
His name is Keum Seok-jung.

508
00:39:01,630 --> 00:39:03,466
He's from Kyeam-ri, Suwon.

509
00:39:12,141 --> 00:39:13,100
Kyeam-ri?

510
00:39:13,684 --> 00:39:17,146
Yes, these two are childhood friends
from their hometown in Kyeam-ri.

511
00:39:25,905 --> 00:39:27,156
That'll be all for today.

512
00:39:27,239 --> 00:39:28,991
We haven't found anything out.

513
00:39:29,075 --> 00:39:32,536
He seems fluent in Japanese.
We don't need an interpreter anymore.

514
00:39:34,038 --> 00:39:36,415
Take these two home in my car.

515
00:39:36,499 --> 00:39:37,333
Yes, sir.

516
00:39:45,299 --> 00:39:46,717
Let's go, Ms. Keum.

517
00:39:49,387 --> 00:39:50,388
Okay.

518
00:40:03,734 --> 00:40:04,693
Keum Seo-hee.

519
00:40:10,658 --> 00:40:11,951
Is your madam

520
00:40:13,911 --> 00:40:15,663
really that good to you?

521
00:40:16,455 --> 00:40:18,165
- Shut up!
- On your way home,

522
00:40:19,542 --> 00:40:22,253
buy a castella cake.

523
00:40:22,336 --> 00:40:23,337
Shut up!

524
00:40:26,465 --> 00:40:27,383
You need to leave.

525
00:40:46,277 --> 00:40:50,614
My daughter is so bright and frugal.

526
00:40:51,449 --> 00:40:54,326
Our butler has been staying in bed
because of a cold

527
00:40:54,410 --> 00:40:56,203
and took three days off.

528
00:40:56,287 --> 00:41:00,708
She made sure to remind me
to deduct three days from his paycheck.

529
00:41:02,668 --> 00:41:04,920
She's that kind of girl.

530
00:41:11,510 --> 00:41:13,596
Sir, the chief of police is on the phone.

531
00:41:13,679 --> 00:41:14,597
He says it's urgent.

532
00:41:15,639 --> 00:41:16,640
Excuse me for a moment.

533
00:41:16,724 --> 00:41:17,892
Sure.

534
00:41:23,898 --> 00:41:24,982
What is it?

535
00:41:29,278 --> 00:41:30,112
What?

536
00:41:54,720 --> 00:41:56,305
What did he say at the end?

537
00:41:59,141 --> 00:42:00,309
I couldn't hear him.

538
00:42:00,392 --> 00:42:01,685
You couldn't hear him?

539
00:42:01,769 --> 00:42:04,563
I was going to ask what he said.

540
00:42:04,647 --> 00:42:05,731
But I got kicked out.

541
00:42:09,318 --> 00:42:10,736
Got it.

542
00:42:12,404 --> 00:42:13,405
So…

543
00:42:15,658 --> 00:42:19,036
What's going to happen to him now?

544
00:42:19,620 --> 00:42:20,621
He'll be put on trial.

545
00:42:21,497 --> 00:42:23,707
How many years is he going to get?

546
00:42:25,501 --> 00:42:28,879
An attempted assassination alone
would get him at least ten years.

547
00:42:30,047 --> 00:42:32,716
On top of that, he killed and wounded
several police officers.

548
00:42:32,800 --> 00:42:35,094
He seriously injured
my predecessor, Mr. Suzuki,

549
00:42:35,177 --> 00:42:36,470
and tried to kill you too.

550
00:42:39,098 --> 00:42:40,891
He could very well be sentenced to death.

551
00:42:45,688 --> 00:42:47,982
By the way, isn't Keum Seo-hee
your real name?

552
00:42:52,861 --> 00:42:53,696
What?

553
00:42:53,779 --> 00:42:55,656
Then what's your real name?

554
00:43:01,078 --> 00:43:01,954
Lee…

555
00:43:03,038 --> 00:43:04,164
Gae-ttong.

556
00:43:05,374 --> 00:43:07,585
- What?
- That's my real name.

557
00:43:07,668 --> 00:43:09,003
Lee Gae-ttong.

558
00:43:10,045 --> 00:43:12,631
But now I'm Keum Seo-hee.

559
00:43:14,717 --> 00:43:17,970
Please, don't tell anyone
at the Government-General about my name.

560
00:43:18,053 --> 00:43:18,887
Promise me.

561
00:43:21,223 --> 00:43:25,853
And that's how you repay someone
who's been so good to you?

562
00:43:32,234 --> 00:43:34,153
What did I do?

563
00:43:35,571 --> 00:43:39,825
You don't know anything
about my relationship with her.

564
00:43:39,908 --> 00:43:41,577
I know more than enough.

565
00:43:42,828 --> 00:43:44,747
You're having an affair
behind someone who's good to you.

566
00:43:45,581 --> 00:43:48,792
You confessed your feelings to another man
behind your lover's back.

567
00:43:49,710 --> 00:43:51,587
Who knows that better than I do?

568
00:43:55,257 --> 00:43:57,635
You seem really hung up on what I said.

569
00:43:57,718 --> 00:44:00,179
Wouldn't it bother you
if you were in my shoes?

570
00:44:01,138 --> 00:44:04,058
You say things like that to anyone,
so I suppose it means nothing--

571
00:44:04,141 --> 00:44:05,934
I don't say that to just anyone.

572
00:44:07,144 --> 00:44:10,230
I only said it to you.
For the first time in my life.

573
00:44:14,443 --> 00:44:15,653
So are you saying…

574
00:44:17,529 --> 00:44:20,240
Mr. Yoo is seeing someone
who doesn't even like him?

575
00:44:22,117 --> 00:44:23,702
Why are you so upset?

576
00:44:24,745 --> 00:44:25,663
What?

577
00:44:25,746 --> 00:44:29,249
Does it bother you that a woman
who's having an affair likes you?

578
00:44:29,333 --> 00:44:31,460
Is that why you're so offended?

579
00:44:33,462 --> 00:44:35,005
Or maybe,

580
00:44:35,756 --> 00:44:39,218
do you hate the thought of me

581
00:44:39,843 --> 00:44:40,678
being with Mr. Yoo?

582
00:44:46,475 --> 00:44:48,310
Should I stop seeing him?

583
00:44:48,394 --> 00:44:50,604
If you tell me to, I will.

584
00:44:50,688 --> 00:44:53,190
Will you believe
my feelings are genuine then?

585
00:44:57,945 --> 00:45:00,989
If I stop seeing Mr. Yoo,

586
00:45:01,490 --> 00:45:04,618
I'll be fired from the Government-General,
get thrown out on the street,

587
00:45:04,702 --> 00:45:06,578
and might never see you again.

588
00:45:07,579 --> 00:45:09,998
I'd hate not to see you again,

589
00:45:10,082 --> 00:45:12,918
but if that's what you want, I'll do it.

590
00:45:21,510 --> 00:45:22,344
Should I

591
00:45:23,470 --> 00:45:25,931
end things with Mr. Yoo?

592
00:45:28,225 --> 00:45:29,184
If I do,

593
00:45:31,019 --> 00:45:33,147
will you accept how I feel about you?

594
00:45:37,568 --> 00:45:39,194
Should I break up with him?

595
00:45:41,905 --> 00:45:42,740
Yes.

596
00:45:45,242 --> 00:45:46,118
Sounds good.

597
00:45:46,618 --> 00:45:49,121
While we're at it,
let's go see Mr. Yoo now.

598
00:45:49,204 --> 00:45:51,915
Break up with him there.
Let's go. Lead the way.

599
00:45:53,041 --> 00:45:53,959
Come on.

600
00:45:55,377 --> 00:45:56,545
What's wrong?

601
00:45:56,628 --> 00:45:57,671
Can't you do it?

602
00:45:59,673 --> 00:46:01,759
See? You can't break up with him.

603
00:46:03,302 --> 00:46:04,428
If you never meant it,

604
00:46:04,511 --> 00:46:07,014
how could you talk
about breaking up that easily?

605
00:46:07,806 --> 00:46:08,640
Well, of course,

606
00:46:09,767 --> 00:46:11,894
saying you like someone is a game to you.

607
00:46:11,977 --> 00:46:13,729
Watch what you say.

608
00:46:13,812 --> 00:46:14,730
Me?

609
00:46:16,440 --> 00:46:18,776
Weren't you the one who spoke
without thinking?

610
00:46:19,568 --> 00:46:22,905
You don't mean a word you say, but you go,
"I like you" or "Should I break up?"

611
00:46:23,655 --> 00:46:25,657
People like you make me sick.

612
00:46:26,366 --> 00:46:28,494
People who throw words around…

613
00:46:32,915 --> 00:46:34,374
with no intention of standing by them.

614
00:46:52,017 --> 00:46:53,143
Welcome, sir!

615
00:46:56,146 --> 00:46:57,314
Is he here?

616
00:46:57,397 --> 00:46:58,232
Yes, sir.

617
00:47:06,740 --> 00:47:09,117
You must've waited a long time
to see me alone.

618
00:47:10,452 --> 00:47:11,829
Are you happy now?

619
00:47:12,704 --> 00:47:13,539
Keum Seok-jung.

620
00:47:15,624 --> 00:47:17,000
Has it been 13 years?

621
00:47:20,671 --> 00:47:22,381
Seeing you again,

622
00:47:23,340 --> 00:47:24,508
I think I remember you.

623
00:47:26,260 --> 00:47:29,805
Ishikawa Nobuyuki.

624
00:47:32,474 --> 00:47:33,892
A mass murderer.

625
00:47:36,562 --> 00:47:39,106
You killed my family, my friends,

626
00:47:40,357 --> 00:47:43,110
and everyone in my village,
then burned them all.

627
00:47:45,445 --> 00:47:46,864
You're a mass murderer.

628
00:47:51,368 --> 00:47:52,619
Mass murder?

629
00:47:52,703 --> 00:47:55,289
I was simply carrying out
my official duty.

630
00:47:55,372 --> 00:47:56,707
I was putting down a riot.

631
00:47:57,749 --> 00:47:58,959
A riot?

632
00:48:01,044 --> 00:48:03,839
All we did was peacefully cry out
for our independence.

633
00:48:03,922 --> 00:48:07,134
We demanded our stolen nation back.

634
00:48:07,217 --> 00:48:08,635
And what did you do?

635
00:48:10,262 --> 00:48:12,806
You locked us up.

636
00:48:17,895 --> 00:48:19,688
<i>You shot us and burned us alive.</i>

637
00:48:40,208 --> 00:48:42,544
In retaliation
for the independence movement.

638
00:48:43,170 --> 00:48:44,338
Retaliation?

639
00:48:46,048 --> 00:48:46,882
No.

640
00:48:48,842 --> 00:48:50,135
Japan doesn't have time

641
00:48:50,802 --> 00:48:54,014
to bother with retaliating
against uncivilized Joseon people.

642
00:48:55,557 --> 00:48:57,601
I was cleared of all charges in court.

643
00:48:59,019 --> 00:49:00,938
I guess you never saw the verdict.

644
00:49:01,605 --> 00:49:05,984
Are you denying everything you ordered
with your own mouth?

645
00:49:12,157 --> 00:49:14,618
Aren't you afraid of being haunted

646
00:49:16,036 --> 00:49:17,537
by those who died that day?

647
00:49:24,795 --> 00:49:28,507
Imagine how much your wife resented you.

648
00:49:31,635 --> 00:49:34,388
Did it ever cross your mind
that it was all your fault?

649
00:49:35,931 --> 00:49:39,309
Have you never thought about
how all those people who died that day

650
00:49:40,894 --> 00:49:42,062
must resent you?

651
00:49:45,732 --> 00:49:50,112
If you hadn't lured those naive people
into your little independence protest,

652
00:49:51,947 --> 00:49:53,490
they'd all still be alive.

653
00:49:55,325 --> 00:49:58,412
And you'd still be with your family,

654
00:49:59,371 --> 00:50:02,124
enjoying a happy life together.

655
00:50:05,711 --> 00:50:06,837
Happy?

656
00:50:09,297 --> 00:50:10,924
Did you just say "happy"?

657
00:50:13,135 --> 00:50:15,137
In a country that's been stolen from us?

658
00:50:15,804 --> 00:50:16,722
Happy?

659
00:50:43,665 --> 00:50:46,418
Those were gunshots, right?

660
00:50:58,472 --> 00:51:00,599
They must've been quite the loving couple,

661
00:51:01,641 --> 00:51:03,769
dying side by side at the same time.

662
00:51:07,022 --> 00:51:08,899
You know, your friend from Kyeam-ri.

663
00:51:10,275 --> 00:51:11,234
What was his name?

664
00:51:11,818 --> 00:51:12,652
Na…

665
00:51:12,736 --> 00:51:15,030
Na whatever-his-name-was?

666
00:51:17,115 --> 00:51:19,409
Ishikawa!

667
00:51:24,706 --> 00:51:26,792
Aren't you afraid of karma?

668
00:51:28,752 --> 00:51:30,921
Mark my words!

669
00:51:32,964 --> 00:51:36,051
You will pay dearly for what you've done.

670
00:51:36,134 --> 00:51:37,552
Don't worry.

671
00:51:39,638 --> 00:51:42,057
I'm not going to kill you.

672
00:51:43,517 --> 00:51:44,559
Your cause of death…

673
00:51:47,437 --> 00:51:48,396
will be an illness.

674
00:51:51,983 --> 00:51:52,943
Fukuda!

675
00:52:07,415 --> 00:52:08,250
Ms. Keum.

676
00:52:09,251 --> 00:52:10,418
Listen to me carefully.

677
00:52:10,502 --> 00:52:12,754
Whatever you're assuming,
you've got it wrong.

678
00:52:13,547 --> 00:52:15,966
He's going to face trial,
which will take months.

679
00:52:16,049 --> 00:52:16,925
So…

680
00:52:17,801 --> 00:52:18,635
Ms. Keum!

681
00:52:20,262 --> 00:52:21,179
Keum Seo-hee!

682
00:53:30,081 --> 00:53:31,374
Interpreter Trainee Keum.

683
00:53:39,674 --> 00:53:41,593
Great work interpreting
the interrogation today.

684
00:53:42,677 --> 00:53:44,012
But I have some bad news.

685
00:53:44,804 --> 00:53:47,015
The suspect just died.

686
00:53:48,808 --> 00:53:51,061
It looks like his gunshot wound
led to sepsis.

687
00:53:58,026 --> 00:53:59,110
I see.

688
00:54:02,030 --> 00:54:03,365
That's a shame.

689
00:54:05,700 --> 00:54:07,869
What a weak man he was.

690
00:54:11,539 --> 00:54:12,749
If you'll excuse me.

691
00:54:59,796 --> 00:55:03,717
The chief of police told me
to drive you both home.

692
00:55:06,469 --> 00:55:07,429
Get in.

693
00:55:08,596 --> 00:55:09,723
They'll take you home.

694
00:55:11,266 --> 00:55:12,100
I'm okay.

695
00:55:13,685 --> 00:55:15,103
I'll go on my own.

696
00:55:49,888 --> 00:55:51,264
You call this okay?

697
00:55:54,726 --> 00:55:55,727
Look at you.

698
00:55:56,227 --> 00:55:57,812
Your hands are shaking.

699
00:56:08,031 --> 00:56:10,075
We've got the Na Byeong-sun couple
down as suicides,

700
00:56:10,158 --> 00:56:13,453
and Keum Seok-jung
as dying of acute sepsis.

701
00:56:14,621 --> 00:56:15,455
Very well.

702
00:56:16,456 --> 00:56:17,916
Sir, there's one thing.

703
00:56:19,542 --> 00:56:22,462
Keum Seo-hee saw Keum Seok-jung's body.

704
00:56:23,046 --> 00:56:24,255
Will that be all right?

705
00:56:24,756 --> 00:56:26,508
She saw the dead body?

706
00:56:26,591 --> 00:56:27,717
Yes, sir.

707
00:56:27,801 --> 00:56:30,887
She came to the interrogation room
with Hideo out of the blue.

708
00:56:32,722 --> 00:56:33,556
With Hideo?

709
00:56:33,640 --> 00:56:34,724
Yes, sir.

710
00:56:35,642 --> 00:56:37,560
I told her he died of septic shock.

711
00:56:42,232 --> 00:56:43,483
But since she saw it,

712
00:56:44,317 --> 00:56:46,236
if Yoo Philip hears about it,

713
00:56:46,319 --> 00:56:49,072
it could turn into a big problem
like the Kyeam-ri incident.

714
00:56:50,824 --> 00:56:53,868
Back then, a foreign reporter
got word out to the foreign press,

715
00:56:54,494 --> 00:56:56,579
and that's how we ended up in court.

716
00:57:01,292 --> 00:57:03,753
Things are already complicated
with Manchuria,

717
00:57:03,837 --> 00:57:05,964
and Westerners won't stop talking.

718
00:57:06,464 --> 00:57:07,424
I'm afraid

719
00:57:08,216 --> 00:57:10,677
they'll start digging up Kyeam-ri
all over again--

720
00:57:10,760 --> 00:57:11,594
Fukuda.

721
00:57:11,678 --> 00:57:12,804
Yes, sir.

722
00:57:14,055 --> 00:57:16,641
Keum Seok-jung died of an illness
during interrogation.

723
00:57:17,350 --> 00:57:19,602
What does that have to do with Yoo Philip?

724
00:57:22,021 --> 00:57:22,897
My mistake, sir.

725
00:57:23,815 --> 00:57:25,984
I got confused for a moment.

726
00:57:29,487 --> 00:57:30,738
On another note,

727
00:57:32,157 --> 00:57:34,784
did you know Keum is a rare family name?

728
00:57:36,035 --> 00:57:36,870
Pardon?

729
00:57:39,247 --> 00:57:40,874
Call the Suwon Police Station.

730
00:57:41,374 --> 00:57:44,377
Have them find Keum Seok-jung's
personal records and send them over.

731
00:57:44,919 --> 00:57:46,129
There should be a record

732
00:57:47,213 --> 00:57:48,673
from 13 years ago.

733
00:57:50,258 --> 00:57:51,092
Yes, sir.

734
00:58:00,852 --> 00:58:02,270
Keum Seok-jung.

735
00:58:03,855 --> 00:58:04,898
Keum Seo-hee.

736
00:58:34,052 --> 00:58:34,886
What?

737
00:58:37,430 --> 00:58:38,473
You're lonely?

738
00:58:39,641 --> 00:58:43,019
Koharu, how can I say that here?

739
00:58:43,978 --> 00:58:46,105
I told you I'm at work.

740
00:58:48,358 --> 00:58:49,192
What?

741
00:58:50,818 --> 00:58:52,195
I said I can't.

742
00:58:53,154 --> 00:58:55,406
I really can't.

743
00:58:57,325 --> 00:58:59,035
Why won't you stop?

744
00:59:08,294 --> 00:59:09,754
You're so silly.

745
00:59:18,763 --> 00:59:20,098
We're here.

746
00:59:37,407 --> 00:59:38,783
Thank you…

747
00:59:40,660 --> 00:59:41,911
for bringing me home.

748
01:00:27,999 --> 01:00:30,501
How am I supposed to tell her?

749
01:01:23,596 --> 01:01:24,681
Ms. Keum.

750
01:01:31,688 --> 01:01:32,897
Are you okay?

751
01:01:39,195 --> 01:01:40,029
Yes.

752
01:01:42,532 --> 01:01:43,658
I'm fi…

753
01:01:55,795 --> 01:01:56,629
No.

754
01:01:57,797 --> 01:01:59,298
I'm not.

755
01:03:29,096 --> 01:03:32,934
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

756
01:03:33,893 --> 01:03:36,187
<i>I don't belong anywhere anyway.</i>

757
01:03:36,270 --> 01:03:37,146
<i>Why?</i>

758
01:03:37,730 --> 01:03:40,233
Have you had a change of heart
since coming to Joseon?

759
01:03:40,316 --> 01:03:43,152
<i>Ms. Keum, you're a good person.</i>

760
01:03:44,153 --> 01:03:47,406
Look into Keum Seo-hee's record.
Her past, all of it. Leave nothing out.

761
01:03:47,490 --> 01:03:49,700
<i>What happened in the past?</i>

762
01:03:49,784 --> 01:03:52,453
<i>He should have been given a proper trial.</i>

763
01:03:52,537 --> 01:03:53,621
I have no regrets.

764
01:03:53,704 --> 01:03:55,414
<i>Could it be Kyeam-ri?</i>

765
01:03:55,498 --> 01:03:56,874
- A family photo?
<i>- Ms. Keum?</i>

766
01:03:56,958 --> 01:03:58,125
What is that

767
01:03:59,252 --> 01:04:00,169
in your hand?

768
01:04:07,468 --> 01:04:09,470
Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim
